                      <p class=3D"corner-intro">A collection of web design =
and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=0D=
=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <p>An=
dy Bell with pitch perfect bait for me: <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail=
2.com/f/a/uhAcAdyCqZtZSmyeWOOF6g~~/AABEfhA~/9NpBONwfkkuovjXPtzj-vZLTiSvFma-=
6ETxCUM5tzquVITHp3xhbR4eryxpvUWi2xbIdOhij3tw1fj9w-m9TM0IkP6b05eJ-Ky-mM0LP2m=
lQATs3ihYF89bxDDjJqYzh75DiH-T014A5raQgqpq6hA~~">Fun shadow coding challenge=
</a>. Just a simple line across a page, but imagine that line is a <em>ligh=
t source</em>. Squares below the light source have a <code>box-shadow</code=
>. How can we make it so as the squares scroll by, that <code>box-shadow</c=
ode> adjusts itself to honor that =E2=80=9Clight source=E2=80=9D=3F</p>=0A<=
img src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.com/cdn-cg=
i/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/bell_light_box=
es.png" alt=3D"" width=3D"450"/>=0A<p>JavaScript=3F Of course, the all-powe=
rful JavaScript can do this. <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/G=
Ku1PchqdbQK6o2leiWSUg~~/AABEfhA~/4Ye-iwPBPRC8xQd2KMXAsiCGkjUyeUK7ocHflIohBG=
7hSqV_ZwuRiVBF6e_VMYFcJCA6_r_EBP68f1cU17Bq2q8-tLoq_zyJ01EpSoXtOuWgA-ypaDcp4=
H7KaY303L2e_CQD3P1PWeL367ThJ0afl7K42LavacePDRCdBkE2bHf7IyUkk0QwNZ9usbe5-Pml=
">That=E2=80=99s what Andy himself did <em>eight years ago</em></a> with th=
e inception of this idea.</p>=0A<p><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com=
/f/a/uSXtUKqOdLqhUk6v5ePq7g~~/AABEfhA~/OPN3pVmZ1ixOBVOkBWyVXkGFG7FFEZA2i4fw=
Flb46zxgTNZzQ-u2pVBoLMoPGtRsM7Yrp2-_NreIY2wwSdI7aBfHk2S5vnov3NjLWFmtUcnpAdT=
l5QaUELyS6BvmNC75eW6U4-eDQSTBeCaGyBOOzxLZsXIhM315ZUpREmGn0jE~">Kitty Giraud=
el blogged in</a> with a solution involving <code>animation-timeline: scrol=
l();</code> which I=E2=80=99m quite sure is just the kind of thing Andy was=
 fishing for. But Kitty relied on some information from JavaScript:</p>=0A<=
blockquote><p>In CSS we can=E2=80=99t read <code>offsetTop</code> or <code>=
offsetHeight</code>, so we pass them from JavaScript as custom properties</=
p></blockquote>=0A<p>This isn=E2=80=99t illegal, of course.</p>=0A<blockquo=
te><p>I think this is one of these things where some JavaScript is not only=
 needed, but also probably quite important to craft a solution that makes f=
ewer assumptions and work nicely in more flexible circumstances =E2=80=94 e=
ven if still relying on scroll-driven animations.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>But=
 certainly it=E2=80=99s fun to flex those muscles a little harder and see i=
f there is a way. <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/AcI-JfkqmOWY=
KcWL8ezLPg~~/AABEfhA~/m3rWKWh_UO-l5v-oWj8iqLTA1BuK9i1W_6NOFEYsusJOs0uddmbAh=
qxG8Vw6EbM8G-xSz3dXD27am0Mk3KC-epoxZWmd-E4FmumEdINFANnWDPmdNkeRckLvY3di3x1T=
TB9oibq_VletnAybMkhvQw~~">Roman Komarov jumped in</a> and used scroll-drive=
n animations as well, but of the <code>animation-timeline: view();</code> t=
ype rather than <code>scroll()</code>. I think this is key as then we=E2=80=
=99ve unlocked information about the boxes position within the viewport tha=
t we wouldn=E2=80=99t have otherwise. I kinda love Roman=E2=80=99s solution=
 as it doesn=E2=80=99t adjust individual <code>box-shadow</code> values at =
all, those just adjust from a shadow generally =E2=80=9Cpointed down=E2=80=
=9D to =E2=80=9Cpointed up=E2=80=9D and it interpolates between them. All t=
he logic is tucked into the <code>animation-range</code> declaration.</p>=
=0A<p><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/nnC_acqIWW0DRLcYf8Q41Q~~=
/AABEfhA~/a7ynJhZsGJU2dAL3DngQcpOmK7nRLlI0dWyrKKVCbCEYl03ho-e-59v8JW31B24Y_=
ClAdmo-UyW4SZCbJ8T8qUgTZgZqxrBx_zfZLkjWsoHal15gBOlhvAU2WqCtytGPtY-dZGRKCDGR=
OsiExX1ZBg~~">There is a take where the box-shadow values are manipulated d=
irectly</a> with a <code>view()</code> scroll timeline though, from Chris B=
olson. Each of the shadows properties are directly manipulated in the keyfr=
ame. Like others, this requires the custom properties to be <code>@property=
</code> delcared with a type so that the animation knows how to animate the=
m. Chris=E2=80=99 approach is extra classy as it has this touch where the f=
urther away from the light source, the lighter the shadow, which feels grea=
t.</p>=0A<p>Chris Wachtman jumped in with some alternate takes based on the=
 light source idea. Like what if the light source wasn=E2=80=99t a line, <a=
 href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/2i7Y2ecZlpKzsvLrOVGuGg~~/AABEfhA=
~/u-IsAzU8OKewaEvp2QHmxYlVKBQjs-geozNKKs1i8IR-vjwG0bPbABCUCNMgRby1JNLQFBZOQ=
FmCqTi1c2X9fxWvRJ9RtnsVdpj1ZR_cLQW7drj6pwnbnCqMW-q2tpXeTc5kSV5Szd4HTAS1J9ev=
FQ~~">but instead your cursor turned into a little sun!</a></p>=0A<p>CSS is=
 so cool. Kitty, Chris(es), Roman, you=E2=80=99re pretty cool too.</p>=0D=
